usage: cp_verify <config folder> <ivk folder> <request file> <y file> <proof file> -id <64 hex> [-bits <asked>]
    [-no-proof-of-work] [-j <threads>] [-q] [-show-stages] [-next-token]
    [-policy <kind>,<C>,<theta>,<cap>,<s>,<sh>]
  one call checks ivk's key.bin, the entry ivk/leaf/<k>.bin that the proof names, and the proof
  -id: the commitment's ID, which names the key's table and the three files of the config folder together. Take it
    from where the commitment is published, or from whoever vouches for the model, before you send your request,
    and never from the files that you were sent. Each stage forms the ID of the files, once it has planned from the
    config folder, and compares it before it looks at anything else of key, entry, request or proof; files with
    another ID end NO: model-statement, and their own ID is not printed.
    Without -id every stage runs its other checks and the call ends at best with NOT-A-VERIFICATION: a YES about a
    key that nobody has named would say nothing. Such a call prints not-shown-without-id in place of the files' ID
  <config folder>: your copy of policy.txt, KL.bin and layout.txt, which in practice the provider writes and -id
    names; without layout.txt every call ends NO: plan-layout, except one that lists the arrays (-plan arrays,
    -plan request -arrays)
  <request file>: exactly T = 2^lt token ids, then r and e (r < e <= T); <y file>: one token id; 32-bit
    little-endian words. The proof covers positions r to e - 1, and position t is about token t + 1. It states
    that tokens r + 1 to min(e, T - 1) meet the acceptance policy set by C, theta and cap in the parameter file
    and, if e = T, that y is the argmax of the last position. If e < T, y must be 0. The answer is about these
    positions alone: it says nothing of any other token and, if e < T, nothing of y. The line "covered:" near the
    end repeats r and e: see that they are the ones you mean
  -next-token: the request must have r = T - 1 and e = T (the proof is about y alone); with other r, e the call
    ends at best with NOT-A-VERIFICATION
  -policy: the acceptance policy that you expect. <kind> is exact-match (C = theta = 2^sh, cap = 0, sT = sh - 4:
    every covered token is the model's first choice) or clipped-gap (sT = sh - 4, other values); the five
    numbers are the lines C, theta, cap, s and sh, in decimal. The last line is NO: policy
    if your parameter file sets another policy than the named one or a kind other than these two (sT is not
    sh - 4), if none is named and a token of the request is covered under a policy other than exact-match, or if
    arithmetic on the named numbers and the request's T, r and e shows that a result would mean nothing; the line
    before the last says why. Under clipped-gap an answer can say less, down to nothing, and the last line of a
    passing call that covers a token of the request is "WITHIN LIMITS: ..", not YES. A covered token has a cost:
    its gap plus 2^sh. The gap is the largest, over all token ids v, of v's logit minus the token's, plus 1 if v is
    the lower id: a token that ties for the largest logit with a lower id has the gap 1, not 0. The answer says that
    the costs, each counted as at most C, have a mean of at most theta, and that at most cap of them lie above C.
    The line "covered:" repeats the five numbers, names the kind that the file sets (policy=) and says how many
    of the covered tokens may cost more than C (above-clip=). Such a token may be any token, and so may others,
    where the model's logits lie close together: the number is no limit on them
  -bits: the requested bits, 0 to 1000000; without it 128. Every round's error must be at most 2^-asked, proof of work
    counted (the line "proof of work: .." says how much); half a digest's bits are 128, so a call above 128 is refused.
    Below 128 every stage runs its checks and the call ends at best with NOT-A-VERIFICATION
  -no-proof-of-work: if some round meets the parameter file's target only with its proof of work counted, every stage
    runs its checks and the call ends at best with NOT-A-VERIFICATION. Work that the file declares and no round needs
    changes nothing. The option changes no number of that file: it holds under whatever parameter file
    is given
  -j: 1 to 256 threads for the request's stage; the two shorter stages take an eighth of that each beside it
  -q: leave out most progress lines
  -show-stages: print the stages' output also if the call does not pass. After a rejection it holds values read from
    the rejected files: use it to find out which check failed, and trust or copy nothing in it
  last line and status:
    YES, 0
    WITHIN LIMITS: <the policy>, 0: as YES, with a token of the request checked under the policy that you named,
      other than exact-match. It is no YES; the status is the same, so tell the two apart by the last line
    NO: <name>, 1
    NOT-A-VERIFICATION, 14
    a bad command line (this text, whose last line says what was not accepted, or a sentence on -id or -policy), or a
      line that begins input-unreadable- (a file could not be read), 2
    no last line, 15: the answer could not be written to standard output
    NOT CHECKED: internal-<..> or <sub-proof>:internal-<..>, 5: a call that ends so has verified nothing. Under
      internal-stage and internal-exception-parFor the machine may be the cause (a want of memory, for one): run it
      again. If it ends so again, the cause may be the program: a guard of the library's ends a stage by a signal,
      which goes under internal-stage, and leaves its line on standard error.
      Under any other name a guard of the verifier's own spoke, and on the same files the call ends so again
    Before YES, WITHIN LIMITS, PART PASSED and NOT-A-VERIFICATION stands the line "proof of work: ..", which says
    whether some round holds at the parameter file's target (sec) only with its proof of work: in how many rounds, the
    most bits of work that the file declares, and the bits of the round with the largest error, once with the work
    counted and once with it left out (not always the same round). Each is a single round's figure, and neither is a
    security level. If you want the checks alone to carry the target, pass -no-proof-of-work
    In a whole call the line "commitment: .." stands right before the line on proof of work: how many closeness rounds
    of the commitment's own files, key and entry, rest on another source than the bound within a third of the distance
    In a whole call one line for programs, which begins "result-v1 ", stands between the line on proof of work and the
    last line. It says what the answer is about, as named fields in a fixed order. Exit status 0 alone is no reason to
    accept an answer: compare the fields with your own values (the verifier's README lists them and what to compare)
    If a stage rejects, the call prints only a heading, that stage's name with the check that failed, and
    NO: <name>, not the stages' output (-show-stages prints it); under another ID, two lines "id:" and the NO
parts, for the committer's own test; they never say YES (PART PASSED: key or leaf, status 3):
  cp_verify -key <config folder> <key.bin> [-id <64 hex>] [-bits <asked>] [-no-proof-of-work] [-j <threads>]
    [-q] [-show-stages]
  cp_verify -leaf <config folder> <key.bin> <entry file> [-id <64 hex>] [-bits <asked>] [-no-proof-of-work]
    [-j <threads>] [-q] [-show-stages]
    -leaf takes the key's table from key.bin's first bytes, on its form alone, and looks at none of the key's
    proofs: its pass counts only beside a -key that passed on the same key.bin, config folder, bits and build
plans, which check nothing (PLAN ONLY, status 4):
  cp_verify -plan id <config folder> <key.bin>
    prints, for the publisher, the line "commitment ID: .." for these files, and h_par=, the digest of the
    three files of the config folder alone (-plan request prints h_par= too). If the files were sent to you, do
    not pass that ID as -id
  cp_verify -plan request <policy.txt> <KL.bin> <asked> [-layout <layout.txt>]
    -prompt <request file> -reply <y file> [-shapes] [-fields] [-arrays] [-j <threads>] [-q]
  cp_verify -plan accounting <config folder> <asked>
    prints the accounting of a whole call under these files (lines "accounting:") and the greatest lengths of key,
    entry and proof; the provers print it through this call
  a plan of request or accounting prints a line "work: <bits> + <work> (..)" if all rounds hold at the parameter
    file's target (sec) and some round only with its proof of work, whatever <asked> is: a checking call under
    these files will count proof of work
  cp_verify -plan arrays <config folder> <asked>
    lists the arrays that a request's sub-proofs commit to (lines "array:"), from policy.txt and KL.bin alone, for
    the tool that makes layout.txt (prover/tools/pack_layout.py --arrays)
research code: nothing is proved about it and no security level is claimed
cp_verify: this command line was not accepted: argument 6 is not an option of this call, comes a second time, or lacks its value
